															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1404533&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609491644" alt="Zyn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1404533)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;" >Zyn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609393008 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">31 Dec 2020 05:36</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4857968">
									

<p>Got a PM about this, took a look. I'll be honest, this kind of seems like <a href="/scp-1655">SCP-1655</a> (Sorrow Tick), but overpowered to an extreme degree.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I am admittedly skeptical of nearly everything in the initial setup:</p>
<ul>
<li>remember the difference between sentient and sapient. Plenty of animals are considered sentient&#8212;if you want human-level intelligence and self-awareness, use &quot;sapient&quot;.</li>
<li>it seems odd that this extradimensional entity just happened to both be capable of taking a human form and also blending in with human society well enough so as to not be immediately noticed as not human.</li>
<li>it also feels like a stretch that the individual just happened to have been picked up by the Foundation as a D-Class.</li>
<li>it also feels like a stretch that the Foundation would have just let this thing in &quot;frequent close proximity of various other anomalies&quot;, especially given that the individual was seemingly convicted for tons of crimes which would make it unreliable at best, completely useless at worst, as a test subject who would be expected to take instruction as part of experimental protocol.</li>
</ul>
<p>The rest of the story just seems like a mishmash of various commonly seen cliché story beats that don't really genuinely increase tension so much as artificially force more danger into the narrative simply to increase drama. You've got the spontaneous unexplained security breach (granted, if the reader headcanons the Foundation as not being hyper-competent like I do, there's more wiggle room here for sudden mistakes), the &quot;sole survivor&quot; main character, the obligatory death of a staff member, the &quot;is it more important/terrible/powerful than we originally thought??&quot; pondering, and the completely unnecessary consideration of using this poorly understood, uncontrollable anomaly for Foundation purposes (I saw in the original post that there was a thought of reclassifying the anomaly as Keter or Thaumiel&#8212;I recommend that you don't do that. The thing isn't Keter if it's not a big deal if no one's close enough for it to mess with, and there's no way the Foundation would ever consider it Thaumiel because it's too unpredictable and untrustworthy to ever be a reliable form of containment. Being potentially useful for keeping something locked up is not the same as being the active containment that keeps something locked up).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Personally I would recommend that you shelve this concept for now, and return to it after you have the experience of a successful article or two written. This is a pretty difficult premise to work with because of how inherently overpowered and unlikable the main entity is (it literally exists to make other people feel bad&#8212;the average reader isn't likely to be emotionally attached to that sort of entity) and how you're intending to portray the Foundation as attributing a lot more significance to the anomaly than makes sense from an objective perspective. Remember, you as the author inherently ascribe value to the entity, but the in-universe Foundation has no obligation to try and grasp at straws to find a use for this thing. Statements like this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the possibility that this new anomaly may be used to injure or possibly destroy other anomalies is cannot be ignored.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>are admittedly difficult for readers to take seriously, since the Foundation has contained tons of dangerous anomalies that could injure or destroy other anomalies, but they're not clamoring to use those either.</p>
<p>If you really want to try writing this, I feel like you would need to at least do the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>don't make this thing an interdimensional traveler. You can have it be alien-ish in origin, but it should have a solid familiarity with Earth/people if it's going to prey on people specifically.</li>
<li>give it more emotional/intellectual depth than &quot;does terrible things, makes people feel bad, because that's what it was conceptualized to do&quot;. Does it have any self-awareness of what has to do to survive? Does it have any redeeming qualities as an individual? This would be the main spot to flesh out, since generic danger effects are pretty common, and often seen in low-rated and deleted works. <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-348">SCPs</a> <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-1468">don't</a> <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-1296">need</a> <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-1171">to</a> <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-2255">be</a> <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-2053">dangerous</a> <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-1481">to</a> <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-1584">be</a> <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-2331">successful</a>.</li>
<li>don't have the Foundation recruit it as a D-Class.</li>
<li>don't have there be a random breach that randomly involves the entity.</li>
<li>remove the unusually insistent focus on this thing's &quot;potential&quot; usefulness.</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
