				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1404533&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609491644" alt="Zyn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1404533)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;" >Zyn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609402193 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">31 Dec 2020 08:09</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4858069">
									

<blockquote>
<p>The idea came to me watching a hypercube building block demonstration. [&#8230;] I imagined how a 4+ dimensional entity would appear to us [&#8230;] This inter-dimensional nature of the beast is the whole point.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't see any of that reflected in the current concept presented. If you want to focus on the wonders of thinking in more than 3 dimensions, don't have the core anomaly focused on the terrible things it does.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>As I understand it D-Class are recruited from death row inmates. Someone convicted of a sadistic murder should be at the top of their list. Additionally, from my reading, D-Class are directly exposed to anomalies all the time</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The issue here is that you're assuming that the in-universe Foundation would conveniently happen to recruit this anomaly as a D-Class, and conveniently test it enough to expose it to multiple anomalies. As a reader, I find the entire setup questionable, since not only is it odd that the Foundation thought that this &quot;sadistic murderer&quot; person was someone they could just lump in with all the other D-Class, they also trusted them enough to expose them to multiple anomalies, rather than one or two. Granted, some readers may suspend their disbelief enough to not be too bothered by this setup, but combined with all the other plot contrivances the storyline for me seems more authorial meddling than believably developing narrative.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It's triggered on purpose by the Anomalous-D while being tasked with routine maintenance. An example might be purposefully breaking eye-contact with SCP-173 while cleaning its cell in order to feed off the trauma of either the creature or the other D-Class in the room</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, if the dude is a sadistic murderer with no sense of self-preservation or empathy, why is the Foundation trusting them with anything? If they really want to use the guy as a lab rat, it would be for protocols with the absolute simplest instructions and plenty of safeguards in case the guy goes rogue. For this case I personally feel like an in-universe researcher would deem the guy not worth the trouble to test on (unless the nature of the anomaly necessitates a test subject who is almost ludicrously volatile and dangerous).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>But they have. The SCP-682-E addendum recounts exactly this. And it makes perfect sense. There are at least two entities that I can think of that are on a termination list and the Foundation doesn't know how they're going to kill them.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>SCP-682 was written almost a decade ago and is one dangerous anomaly out of thousands. Most readers aren't going to take your premise seriously if you need to rely on super-old (and frankly, not great examples of current site standards) Series I articles to support your reasoning for the Foundation acting irrationally.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm also playing with the notion that our thoughts, souls and emotions do exist on planes that we don't have conscious access to. Yet our emotions and souls are tied to our bodies, and in that way we are all extra-dimensional beings.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You might want to consider a tale or GoI format entry then, since the level of non-observable knowledge required to understand this thing empirically seems to necessitate omnipresence that the in-universe Foundation wouldn't have.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I've highlighted its potential because I'm thinking of the stories that follow next. If this entity is to live in isolation it will be just like so many others. It will be just another mystery in a box that says nothing about the larger world and how things work together. To me that is uninteresting.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If the core article is downvoted to deletion range, it doesn't matter how many stories beyond the first page you've planned. Focus on making the SCP entity enjoyable to read as a standalone first, and then if the audience response to it is good then you can start think about branching out. If you try too hard to force openings for additional followup works, it'll just feel contrived and overreaching.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>In general though, I feel like your plans for the page are way more complicated and large-scale than I personally would enjoy reading about. At this point I think your best bet is to reach out to authors of high-rated extradimensional SCP articles (you can find them with the <a href="/tag-search">Tag Search</a>) and see what they think of what you have planned.</p>

							</div>

								
					
