<p>As a reader, I find the entire setup questionable, since not only is it odd that the Foundation thought that this &quot;sadistic murderer&quot; person was someone they could just lump in with all the other D-Class,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have to admit, I find this confusing. I thought D-Class came off of death row. Who goes to death row but murderers? Lumping this disposable murder in with all the other disposable murderers is exactly what I thought the Foundation would do.</p>
<p>Taken directly from the <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/security-clearance-levels">wiki page on classes</a> <em>&quot;&#8230;Class D personnel are expendable personnel used to handle extremely hazardous anomalies and are not allowed to come into contact with Class A or Class B personnel. Class D personnel are typically drawn worldwide from the ranks of prison inmates convicted of violent crimes, especially those on death row.&quot;</em></p>
<p>This describes him perfectly. And that he's repeatedly put in dangerous close contact with anomalies sounds like exactly what they're used for.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-682 was written almost a decade ago and is one dangerous anomaly out of thousands. Most readers aren't going to take your premise seriously if you need to rely on super-old (and frankly, not great examples of current site standards) Series I articles to support your reasoning for the Foundation acting irrationally.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I am new to the concept of the SCP Foundation (learned about it two weeks ago). SCP-682 was one of my entry points into the storytelling. I do admit that I have no perspective on what people who have been immersed in it for years think of that particular anomaly, but as a new reader it honestly never occurred to me that it was in any way outdated.</p>
