			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4858771">
									

<p>Not all D-class come from &quot;death row&quot;. Not all D-class come from the United States. The SCP Foundation is a global organization that pulls staff from all corners of the globe and beyond. Fixating exclusively on how the US does things is unhelpful and not relevant in this context.</p>
<p>Murdermachine SCPs were a staple in earlier forms of the wiki and have more or less dropped off in popularity, despite the prevalence of them in off-site media. The reality is is that they've been done over and over again and the concept is now worn out and boring.</p>
<p>The entire concept is relying heavily on decade old material and doesn't reflect the current atmosphere or environment of the wiki. Part of the greenlight/crit process is to not only help you flesh out your idea/draft, but to also help guide new authors to stuff that works on the site. You can either listen to this advice, or post your thing anyway and let the voting populace decide.</p>
<p>I often tend to think that being willing to adjust an idea in workshop is preferable to having your piece get downvoted to oblivion on the main site.</p>
<p>Finally, I strongly urge you to read the more recent articles on the site to draw inspiration from. A lot of the series-1 articles have been replicated so many times now that people are simply sick of it. Utilizing them as the primary foci of a current piece rarely does well.</p>

