<blockquote>
<p>Not all D-class come from &quot;death row&quot;. Not all D-class come from the United States. The SCP Foundation is a global organization that pulls staff from all corners of the globe and beyond. Fixating exclusively on how the US does things is unhelpful and not relevant in this context.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You know, I was just about to amend what I'd written in response to Zyn above because of this exact point.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>You're discussing the international transportation of people from all over the globe - blasphemers, political prisoners, drug dealers. Some are illiterate. Many won't speak the language at their destination or the language of the other prisoners they're traveling with. At the end of their journey they will be tasked with doing terrifying and extremely dangerous work under exacting conditions.</p>
<p>Against their will.</p>
<p>If, as Zyn proposes, a murderer from the US is deemed too sadistic to work with, would the alternative be an illiterate goat-heard from Saudi Arabia who's been convicted of adultery and speaks no english?</p>
<p>Are there an infinite number of these people, or do compromises ever have to be made?</p>
<p>I ask these questions not to be argumentative, but because these are interesting problems. There are probably several interesting stories here. Opportunities to flesh out the cannon.</p>
<p>This interests me. It's stories like this I want to work on.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Part of the greenlight/crit process is to not only help you flesh out your idea/draft, but to also help guide new authors to stuff that works on the site.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But that's not what's happening here.</p>
<p>We're here debating matters of fact. Who does and does not go on death row.</p>
<p>Help fleshing it out would be great.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I often tend to think that being willing to adjust an idea in workshop is preferable to having your piece get downvoted to oblivion on the main site.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Totally agree. If this discussion is a workshop then I'm 100% on board.</p>
<p>Some of the things I'd want to ask in workshop are where have these concepts been tried before, what worked and what didn't? Is any part of the concept in direct contradiction of cannon? How does a Scranton Reality Anchor work? Were it done to death or was absolutely impossible for some reason of established cannon, I would drop it or adjust.</p>
<p>My opinions about the kinds of problems I'm interested in probably won't change, but there are many angles from which to attack them. I'd find another.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Finally, I strongly urge you to read the more recent articles on the site to draw inspiration from.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This I am doing.</p>
</div>
