<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>But that's not what's happening here.</p>
<p>We're here debating matters of fact. Who does and does not go on death row.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Some of the things I'd want to ask in workshop are where have these concepts been tried before, what worked and what didn't? Is any part of the concept in direct contradiction of cannon?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>D-Class portrayal is a rather significant part of the Foundation Universe canon at large (the site even had a contest focusing on D-Class: <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/d-class-contest">http://www.scpwiki.com/d-class-contest</a>). If you really want to have your core anomaly be frivolously exposed to multiple anomalies in order to ensure your planned plotline happens, you do so at the risk of readers becoming skeptical of Foundation incompetence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If this discussion is a workshop then I'm 100% on board.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'll be honest, thus far it doesn't look like you're open to adjusting anything but the smallest details of your concept. That combined with what seems to be an insinuation that the discussion doesn't meet your standards for &quot;a workshop&quot; and thus it's the reviewers' fault for you not editing your concept doesn't really encourage readers to want to assist you.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>It's fine if you want to write material solely based on your interests in &quot;fleshing out the canon&quot; with minimal edits&#8212;those works are posted to the Fan Work forum and are not expected to be closely critiqued (if at all).</p>
<p>If you want to post content to the mainsite where it will be voted on by readers who have their own headcanon that may not mesh with your own, you're going to need to adjust your premise rather than trying to convince readers that they need to change their preferences to suit yours. The large majority of our day-to-day readers/voters read for leisure and don't check author posts; your content needs to be compelling without needing you to defend it in several separate discussion posts.</p>

							</div>

								
					
